Your pip isn't misconfigured — it's **half-deleted**. The `pip` package source is gone from site-packages while its metadata remains, so the `pip` launcher script dies on import.

**Evidence (read off the machine, not inferred):**

- `/usr/local/bin/pip` runs under `#!/usr/local/bin/python3.13` — the same interpreter as `python3`, so the usual "pip belongs to a different python" trap is *not* your problem.
- That interpreter's site-packages contains `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` but **no `pip/` directory**. `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` is exactly why the repair tools will tell you pip is "already satisfied" while every `pip install` fails.
- The failure text is a traceback, not a policy refusal:

```
File "/usr/local/bin/pip", line 5, in <module>
    from pip._internal.cli.main import main
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

**Ruled out, so you don't waste time on them:** PEP 668 externally-managed (that marker exists only for the OS Python 3.11 in `/usr/lib`; your active Python is 3.13 in `/usr/local`, which has none), permissions (you're uid 0 and own site-packages), disk space (~145 GB free), SSL (OpenSSL 3.0.22 working), and pip.conf overrides (none on the box).

**Fix — run these yourself; I didn't touch the installation:**

```sh
# Definitive: rebuild pip from the official bootstrap
curl -sS https://bootstrap.pypa.io/get-pip.py -o get-pip.py
python3 get-pip.py
rm get-pip.py
pip --version && pip install --dry-run requests   # both should now succeed
```

If `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` first says "Requirement already satisfied", that's the stale `dist-info` talking — remove `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info` (and any `pip-*.dist-info` beside it) and re-run `ensurepip`, or just use `get-pip.py` above.

One caveat, since you said "system-wide": this box has two Pythons — 3.13 in `/usr/local` (the one `python3`/`pip` resolve to) and the distro's 3.11. If you actually meant the distro one, install its pip through the OS (`apt-get install python3-pip`) instead; the commands above repair 3.13 only.

Two things I did not do: I ran no repair (a diagnostic turn shouldn't mutate a system interpreter), and the raw command output above came from a delegated shell turn rather than my own tools, which have no `bash`.

